WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.941
Here's a question we rarely ask.

2
00:00:04.941 --> 00:00:07.079
Every thread in a pool is busy.

3
00:00:07.079 --> 00:00:09.217
Is that good news, or bad news?

4
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:14.081
Picture the kitchen on a Saturday night, when orders stop coming out.

5
00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:15.284
Which cook is stuck?

6
00:00:15.284 --> 00:00:17.448
They all wear the same white jacket.

7
00:00:17.448 --> 00:00:19.612
Which table was the burned dish for?

8
00:00:19.612 --> 00:00:21.536
The note stayed with the waiter.

9
00:00:21.536 --> 00:00:23.761
How long has the rail been this full?

10
00:00:23.761 --> 00:00:24.843
Nobody kept count.

11
00:00:24.843 --> 00:00:28.931
And at closing, the lights go off with two dishes still in the oven.

12
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:33.745
A thread pool meets the same questions, and its defaults answer none of them.

13
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:35.649
First, name tags.

14
00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:42.735
A thread dump is a snapshot of every thread in a running program: its name, its state, and the line it's on.

15
00:00:42.735 --> 00:00:46.737
The ready-made pools name their threads like pool-3-thread-7.

16
00:00:46.737 --> 00:00:51.264
In a dump with eight pools, that tells you as much as a white jacket.

17
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:59.641
So give the pool a thread factory, the object it asks for new threads, that names them after the work: grill-0, grill-1.

18
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:01.477
Second, the note.

19
00:01:01.477 --> 00:01:08.342
A request id is a short label, like req-42, that a program prints on every log line about one request.

20
00:01:08.342 --> 00:01:14.399
Programs often keep it in a ThreadLocal, a variable with a separate value for each thread.

21
00:01:14.399 --> 00:01:19.581
Picture a note pinned to the waiter's jacket instead of clipped to the order.

22
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:22.489
ContextDemo has a pool of one thread.

23
00:01:22.489 --> 00:01:25.691
main sets req-42, then submits three tasks.

24
00:01:25.691 --> 00:01:28.372
What does the first, plain task see?

25
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:31.546
null.

26
00:01:31.546 --> 00:01:33.832
The note stayed on main's jacket.

27
00:01:33.832 --> 00:01:40.065
And the third task sees req-7, an old note that an earlier task left pinned to the worker.

28
00:01:40.065 --> 00:01:45.883
Now every log line blames request 7, which went home long ago and did nothing wrong.

29
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:53.946
The fix: copy the note onto the ticket at the hand-off, pin it on whichever cook takes it, and take it off when the dish leaves.

30
00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:57.024
In code: capture, install, and restore in finally.

31
00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.785
Here the picture breaks.

32
00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.360
A paper note falls off sooner or later.

33
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.057
A ThreadLocal stays on its thread until code removes it.

34
00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:08.491
So a ThreadLocal belongs to a thread, not to a task.

35
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.997
Third, the board by the pass.

36
00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:13.723
Four numbers tell you most of what you need.

37
00:02:13.723 --> 00:02:15.954
The queue size: tickets on the rail.

38
00:02:15.954 --> 00:02:19.672
The active count: busy cooks, against the most you can have.

39
00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:23.204
Rejections: orders turned away, which you count yourself.

40
00:02:23.204 --> 00:02:26.797
And time in queue: how long each ticket waited for a cook.

41
00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:35.934
MetricsDemo sends 10 tasks of 300 milliseconds to a pool of two threads, named orders, with room for four on the rail.

42
00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:38.445
Predict the first row of the board.

43
00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:47.265
Two active, four queued, four rejected at once, because two plus four is all it can hold.

44
00:02:47.265 --> 00:02:50.230
Then every 300 milliseconds two more finish.

45
00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:53.937
The queue goes four, two, zero, and done climbs to six.

46
00:02:54.133 --> 00:03:00.327
Run the same burst on newFixedThreadPool, whose rail has no end, and rejected says zero.

47
00:03:00.327 --> 00:03:05.324
The last two tickets wait 1.2 seconds before a cook even looks at them.

48
00:03:05.324 --> 00:03:07.295
Only time in queue shows it.

49
00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:11.720
So watch the queue, not just the threads, and count every rejection.

50
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:17.037
A pool with an empty queue and no rejections is healthy, however busy its cooks look.

51
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.176
Now a board where everything froze.

52
00:03:20.176 --> 00:03:25.744
All threads active, the queue full and not moving, nothing done, and the CPU idle.

53
00:03:25.744 --> 00:03:26.695
What happened?

54
00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:29.986
At closing, a job prints every table's bill.

55
00:03:29.986 --> 00:03:36.297
Each bill task submits a fetch of that table's orders to the same pool, and waits for it.

56
00:03:36.297 --> 00:03:44.380
With 20 threads and 400 tables, the first 20 bills take every thread, and their 20 fetches queue behind 380 bills.

57
00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:50.324
In the kitchen, every cook has pinned a ticket at the back of the rail and stands waiting for it.

58
00:03:50.324 --> 00:03:51.936
No cook is left to take it.

59
00:03:51.936 --> 00:03:54.623
Nothing throws, and nothing will ever finish.

60
00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.517
That's pool-induced deadlock: every thread in a bounded pool waits for a task queued in that same pool.

61
00:04:01.517 --> 00:04:08.168
DeadlockDemo's smallest case shows it on two threads: active two, queued two, completed zero, forever.

62
00:04:08.333 --> 00:04:13.120
The fixes: don't wait inside the pool, compose with thenApply instead.

63
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.445
Or fetch inline, in the same task.

64
00:04:15.445 --> 00:04:17.633
Or give each stage its own pool.

65
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:19.662
Last, closing time.

66
00:04:19.662 --> 00:04:27.565
A good restaurant locks the front door first, then finishes its orders, upstream stations first, all before a deadline.

67
00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:29.671
In code: stop taking requests.

68
00:04:29.671 --> 00:04:33.546
Shut down the kitchen pool before the billing pool it feeds.

69
00:04:33.546 --> 00:04:37.937
Give the whole thing one deadline inside the 30 second grace period.

70
00:04:37.937 --> 00:04:40.197
And whatever didn't finish, log it.

71
00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:43.396
Do it backwards, and billing closes first.

72
00:04:43.396 --> 00:04:47.311
The dishes still cooking get cooked and served, and never billed.

73
00:04:47.311 --> 00:04:49.057
A free dinner nobody planned.

74
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.687
So, is a pool with every thread busy good news?

75
00:04:52.687 --> 00:04:54.161
It depends on the board.

76
00:04:54.161 --> 00:04:59.321
Name the cooks, clip the note to the ticket, and watch the queue and the rejections.

77
00:04:59.321 --> 00:05:02.392
And never let a cook wait for a ticket behind him.

78
00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:03.995
Quick check.

79
00:05:03.995 --> 00:05:10.032
The board says: active two of two, queued two, done zero, and the CPU is idle.

80
00:05:10.032 --> 00:05:11.193
Busy, or stuck?

81
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:14.634
Stuck.

82
00:05:14.634 --> 00:05:15.863
Nothing finishes.

83
00:05:15.863 --> 00:05:20.129
Look for a task that waits on another task in its own pool.
